======================================================================
PART 4: CRT-factored view obstruction
======================================================================

CRT factorization of the lonely condition:
  δ = {7t}, θ_a = {at} for a=1,...,6
  Lonely iff:
    δ ∈ [1/14, 13/14]
    For a=1,...,6: θ_a ∈ [1/14, 13/14] ∩ [1/14-δ, 13/14-δ] (mod 1)

Constraint width as function of δ:
  δ=0/14=0.0000: constraint width per class = 0.8571
  δ=1/14=0.0714: constraint width per class = 0.7857
  δ=2/14=0.1429: constraint width per class = 0.7143
  δ=3/14=0.2143: constraint width per class = 0.6429
  δ=4/14=0.2857: constraint width per class = 0.5714
  δ=5/14=0.3571: constraint width per class = 0.5000
  δ=6/14=0.4286: constraint width per class = 0.4286
  δ=7/14=0.5000: constraint width per class = 0.3571
  δ=8/14=0.5714: constraint width per class = 0.2857
  δ=9/14=0.6429: constraint width per class = 0.2143
  δ=10/14=0.7143: constraint width per class = 0.1429
  δ=11/14=0.7857: constraint width per class = 0.0714
  δ=12/14=0.8571: constraint width per class = 0.7143
  δ=13/14=0.9286: constraint width per class = 0.7857
  δ=14/14=1.0000: constraint width per class = 0.8571

Product of class constraint widths as function of δ:
  δ=1/14: width=0.7857, product=0.235282
  δ=2/14: width=0.7143, product=0.132810
  δ=3/14: width=0.6429, product=0.070581
  δ=4/14: width=0.5714, product=0.034815
  δ=5/14: width=0.5000, product=0.015625
  δ=6/14: width=0.4286, product=0.006196
  δ=7/14: width=0.3571, product=0.002075

THE PROOF FRAMEWORK:
  At δ=1/14: each class has constraint width 11/14 ≈ 78.6%.
  The 6 class constraints on (θ₁,...,θ₆) define a BOX of volume (11/14)⁶ ≈ 27.7%.
  The line (t, 2t, 3t, 4t, 5t, 6t) mod 1 passes through this box
  iff a specific linear Diophantine condition is met.

  For INITIAL SEGMENT: at t=1/14, δ={7/14}=1/2, and θ_a = {a/14}.
  Width at δ=1/2: 12/14 - 1/2 = 5/14 per class.
  Product = (5/14)^6 ≈ 0.0002 — tight but nonzero!

  For δ NEAR 0: width ≈ 12/14 per class, product ≈ (6/7)^6 ≈ 37.6% — easy.
  The proof should show that for some δ ∈ [1/14, 13/14],
  the corresponding (θ₁,...,θ₆) constraints are satisfiable.

======================================================================
PART 5: Volume / equidistribution argument
======================================================================

Initial segment speeds {1,...,13}:
  lcm(1,...,13) = 360360
  volume of L = (12/14)^13 = 0.134801
  N × V = 48576.7

  points in L: 6 / 360360
  fraction: 0.00001665
  expected (equidistributed): 0.134801

  primes: partial lcm((1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 11)) = 2310
    points in L: 210 / 2310
    fraction: 0.09090909

  spread: partial lcm((1, 3, 7, 11, 13, 17)) = 51051
    points in L: 5258 / 51051
    fraction: 0.10299504

EQUIDISTRIBUTION ARGUMENT:
  The Weyl sum for the line (v₁t,...,v₁₃t) mod 1:
  S(N) = (1/N) Σ_{k=0}^{N-1} 1_L(v₁k/N,...,v₁₃k/N)
  converges to Vol(L) = 0.134801 as N → ∞.

  For FINITE N = lcm(speeds), S(N) is EXACT.
  Initial segment: S(360360) = 0.00001665
  Equidistributed prediction: 0.13480057
  Ratio: 0.000124

  The ratio is < 1 — the line is
  UNDER-represented in L
  relative to equidistribution.

THE PROOF QUESTION (restated):
  LRC@14 ⇔ for every primitive (v₁,...,v₁₃),
  (1/N) Σ_{k=0}^{N-1} 1_L(v₁k/N,...,v₁₃k/N) > 0
  where N = lcm(v₁,...,v₁₃).

  The equidistribution gives S(N) → V ≈ 13.5% as max(v_i) → ∞.
  The EXTREMAL case is when the speeds are SMALL and the
  line is NOT well-distributed.
  The initial segment (max speed 13) is the tightest case.
  Its S(N) = 6/360360 = 0.00001665.

======================================================================
SYNTHESIS
======================================================================

THE PERMUTOHEDRON PROOF FRAMEWORK:
  LRC@14 = line on T^13 hits box L = [1/14, 13/14]^13.
  Volume(L) = (6/7)^13 ≈ 13.5%.

  THREE APPROACHES:
  A. SLAB REDUNDANCY: 13 slabs of measure 1/7 have total 13/7 ≈ 1.86.
     If pairwise overlaps > 6/7, Bonferroni gives μ(lonely) > 0.
  B. CRT FACTORING: reduce to δ + 6 class constraints. At δ near 0,
     class constraints have width 12/14 and product (6/7)^6 ≈ 37.6%.
  C. EQUIDISTRIBUTION: S(N) → 13.5%. For initial segment,
     S(360360) counts EXACT lonely lattice points.

  THE WINNER should be a HYBRID: use CRT to factor the problem,
  Bonferroni within each CRT factor to bound the class-safe measure,
  and the permutohedron face lattice to bound the cross-class correlation.

